  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1861, after the bombardment of 1851 had already forced the Oba to accept a British presence. 1886 is the Royal Niger Company's charter and 1900 the transfer of its territory to the Crown.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1851. It was the military action that preceded annexation, and the paper sometimes asks for the two dates separately.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1884–85. It produced the principle of effective occupation, which is why Britain moved from trading posts to formal protectorates.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1886, under Sir George Goldie. The company governed the Niger area until the Crown took over its territory in 1900.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Crown took over, and Lugard became High Commissioner of Northern Nigeria. The company had governed by charter; the transfer began direct colonial administration.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1914, by Lord Frederick Lugard, who became Governor-General of the united colony and protectorate. 1906 is the union of Lagos with the Southern Protectorate.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lugard. Clifford, Richards and Macpherson gave their names to later constitutions, not to the amalgamation.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Indirect rule, the policy Lugard developed in Northern Nigeria and later described in The Dual Mandate in British Tropical Africa.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The emirate supplied a ruler at every level — emir, district head, village head — so the British could govern through him. The east lacked that hierarchy.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The warrant chief held a warrant from the colonial government, not a traditional title. His authority was resented because it had no basis in Igbo custom.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The immediate trigger was the rumoured taxation of women, and the deeper grievance was the warrant chief's abuse of power. It is also called the Women's War.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Aba, in the east, and spread through the surrounding districts. The paper sometimes calls it the Aba riots, and the precise name is the Aba Women's Riot or Women's War.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Governor at the top, the Resident in a province, the District Officer in a division, and the Native Authority at the base. The other orders invert the chain.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Resident headed a province and supervised the district officers and the native authorities within it. A district officer headed a division, not a province.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Alkali courts continued under native authority, which is why indirect rule preserved the judicial structure of the emirate as well as its executive.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Colonial administration was expected to pay for itself, and taxation was the instrument. It was also the grievance behind the 1929 women's war.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cocoa, groundnut, palm produce and cotton earned foreign exchange for Britain and paid for the colony's imports. Food crops continued alongside them.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The colonial state left a centralised administration, a common legal system, a common currency and a transport network — along with the regional divisions that fed later conflict.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The economy was organised around the export of primary products and the import of manufactures, a structure the country carried into independence.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The native authority collected taxes, kept order and ran local courts, but under the Resident and the Governor. Its powers were delegated and supervised.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Indirect rule froze traditional institutions in place, gave chiefs powers they had not had before, and made them agents of the colonial state rather than representatives of their people.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lugard argued that the colonial power held the territory in trust both for its people and for the wider world. It is the classic statement of the civilising justification.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1906. The merger created a single southern administration, which was then amalgamated with the north in 1914.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lagos was the capital from 1914 until 1991, when the capital moved to Abuja. Ibadan, Enugu and Kaduna were regional headquarters.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It created the single territorial unit that became Nigeria, and it is the date the paper expects for amalgamation. The regions came in 1946 and the elective principle in 1922.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Igbo village republic had no king, so the British created warrant chiefs who lacked legitimacy — the root of the 1929 crisis.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The chiefs were the base of the administrative pyramid, supervised by British officers. They had no role in foreign policy or in the Westminster Parliament.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The lines ran from the producing areas to the ports — Kano to Lagos, Jos to Port Harcourt — and served the export economy.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nigeria mattered for trade, raw materials and the route to the east. Oil was discovered in 1956, late in the colonial period, and there were no white settler colonies of the Kenyan kind.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    English law applied in the colony and the protectorate, customary law in the south and Sharia in the north, a plural system that survived into independence.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Colonial constitutions were British instruments, made in London and imposed on the territory. The Nigerian role in them grew only from 1946 onwards.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Elected Nigerian ministers with portfolios came with the Macpherson Constitution of 1951. Before that the executive was the Governor and his officials.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Assimilation was the French policy of making colonial subjects into French citizens. British policy was indirect rule, which preserved local institutions under supervision.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The company had governed by charter; from 1900 the British government governed directly through a High Commissioner, which is the beginning of the colonial state proper.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ordinance extended direct taxation, which the north already paid, to the south — and the attempt to tax women in the east produced the 1929 revolt.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The inquiry that followed led to changes in the native authority system, including the replacement of some warrant chiefs and the introduction of clan councils.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Governor, styled Governor-General after 1914, was the supreme authority. The President and Prime Minister belong to the independence era, and the Resident headed a province.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Monoculture exposed the colony to price swings, a vulnerability that survived independence and reappeared with oil.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Indirect rule preserved local institutions under supervision; assimilation sought to remake the subject in the French image. The contrast is a standard examination question.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lugard amalgamated the two protectorates administratively but kept the north and south under separate lieutenant-governors, which is why the regional division persisted.
  ]

]

